methods.description:
1. Extend step_01.ERA5_LAND_BANDS. It already pulls temperature_2m_min from ECMWF/ERA5_LAND/DAILY_AGGR. Add:
- temperature_2m_max;
- dewpoint_temperature_2m, giving ea = e0(Td);
- surface_solar_radiation_downwards_sum (J m-2 to MJ m-2);
- u_component_of_wind_10m and v_component_of_wind_10m;
- surface_pressure.
2. Compute daily PM (FAO-56 eq. 6) in Python with pyet.pm_fao56 or pyfao56 refet:
- u2 = 0.748*u10;
- Rso from eq. 37;
- Rnl from Tmax, Tmin, ea and Rs/Rso.
3. Wind caveat: DAILY_AGGR averages the u and v components separately, so the speed of the vector mean underestimates scalar wind. Either compute the daily mean of hourly |U| from ECMWF/ERA5_LAND/HOURLY, or accept a small bias, since sensitivity is low in humid Gers. Print bandNames() once to confirm band names.
4. Benchmark 1991-2025 against the Meteo-France SIM2 daily 8 km grid (open CSV on data.gouv). Its gauge-based SAFRAN precipitation also checks the ERA5 rainfall, which is the main forcing error.
5. Keep Hargreaves with real Tmax only as a gap-filler, rescaled to PM by a DOY ratio.
6. The same ET0 feeds SPEI-3 (F3 proposal Z9).

(a) Vine Kcb(t): use the FAO-56 trapezoid (0.15/0.65/0.40). Scale Kcb_mid per parcel by STRUCTURAL vigour, i.e. the multi-year median of July-August NDVI, when the southern-France inter-row is senescent:
- fc_vine = (NDVI - NDVI_bg)/(NDVI_full - NDVI_bg), with NDVI_bg about 0.30 and NDVI_full about 0.85;
- Kd (Allen and Pereira 2009) = min(1, ML*fc, fc^(1/(1+h))), with ML 1.5 and h 1.75;
- Kcb_mid = Kc_min + Kd*(Kcb_full - Kc_min).

Current-season updates may only use pre-stress windows (up to flowering, early June) or detected canopy events (hedging, missing vines). Never use the stressed late-summer NDVI, because Ks already carries the stress.

(b) Cover-crop Kcb(t): estimate from S-2 when vines are leafless or small (November to early May). This requires ingesting Nov-Mar 10 m statistics, which today's IV-X filter blocks; no SR is needed. Use the regional grass seasonal shape; in the prototype the ISMN grass-plot NDVI served as the proxy.

(c) Options:
- the spectral-unmixing approach of Guevara-Torres et al. 2025;
- a row-geometry fc (canopy width / row spacing) with the shaded-area relation of Williams and Ayars 2005 (Kc about 0.017 x % shade).

Compute SAVI in GEE as well, so that Campos et al. 2010 (Kcb = 1.79*SAVI - 0.08 or 1.44*NDVI - 0.10) can serve as a benchmark.

methods.why_better_than_ndvi_only:
In VINEYARD_06 the whole-pixel NDVI is dominated by inter-row dynamics:
- an early-May dip from weeding (0.41);
- an August dip from grass senescence;
- a September-October regrowth (0.54-0.61).

A generic NDVI to Kcb mapping therefore saturated in the prototype, with FTSW < 0.4 from early June in every year.

Same-season NDVI also contains the drought response, so using it as Kcb double-counts stress and damps the signal. In the prototype, Kcb_mid was 0.42 in 2022 vs 0.74 in 2021, and the season Spearman with ISMN fell from 0.79 with fixed FAO Kcb to 0.64 (n=9, directional only). VI-based Kcb overestimates actual Kc under stress (Campos 2012) and cannot track stress (Almeida 2024).

methods.validation_plan:
Pre-registered A/B inside M1:
- whole-pixel Kcb;
- FAO fixed;
- structural vigour;
- structural vigour plus cover.

Metrics:
- dry-onset MAE at SMOSMANIA;
- number of saturated seasons;
- inter-annual rank agreement of the season stress index with ISMN and, later, with pilot predawn potential.

Check that Kcb_mid stays within the FAO-56 and Rallo 2021 ranges and the rainfed calibration of Almeida 2024 (0.60).

methods.description:
Per parcel and per dekad, report:
- absolute FTSW with the M4 uncertainty band;
- FTSW z vs 1991-2020;
- stress-intensity integrals per phenological period (budburst-flowering, flowering-veraison, veraison-harvest), with dates from a degree-day model on ERA5 Tmean, following Gaudin et al. 2014 (five deficit classes from period-specific FTSW thresholds anchored to predawn potential);
- days until FTSW < 0.4, from running M1 forward with the 10-day forecast precipitation and ET0 (Open-Meteo/ECMWF is already used by step_06).

Status logic:
- watch = SPI;
- warning = soil anomaly (ERA5-L 0-28 cm SMA <= -1 or FTSW z <= -1);
- new field vine_stress = FTSW below the phase-specific threshold AND an anomaly;
- alert = vine_stress AND an observed canopy response, i.e. the existing S-2 NDVI/NDMI anomaly <= -1 in VI-IX, with the full EDO persistence rules from F3 Z1.

NDVI therefore stays, but as independent confirmation of impact rather than the only parcel-specific information.

methods.validation_plan:
Backtest 2016-2025 with three tests:
(i) vine_stress dekads against ISMN dry dekads (POD, FAR, CSI), using onset dates because summer values are QC-4 censored;
(ii) season index against cooperative yield, sugar and harvest date per parcel, if obtainable;
(iii) days-to-stress hindcast with a perfect forecast (ERA5) first, then archived forecasts, reporting the MAE in days.

Pre-register metrics before the season:
- Spearman of the season stress index with delta13C and yield across parcels;
- MAE of modelled FTSW against FTSW inferred from predawn potential via a Lebon/Pellegrino-type relation;
- POD/FAR of vine_stress against predawn potential < -0.4 MPa dates.

research.top_recommendation:
Build step_08 as a dual-source, two-layer FAO-56 vine water balance (vine + inter-row cover). Present it honestly: as a translation layer into vine units and phenology-aware timing, not as more accurate soil moisture. The prototype shows it does not raise daily R against ISMN, but it tracks the onset of seasonal drying far better (MAE 14-20 d vs 66 d for the current SMA layer).

Order of work:

1. Switch the soil-warning layer to depth-matched ERA5-L 0-28 cm (dR = +0.108 [+0.049, +0.167] at Condom) and add the QC-4 contact-loss flag. About 1 day.

2. Add the Penman-Monteith ERA5-L bands to step_01 and benchmark ET0 and precipitation against Meteo-France SIM2. About 1.5 days.

3. Implement step_08 with:
- FAO-56 wine-grape priors: Kcb 0.15/0.65/0.40; stages 30/60/40/80 d from April; Zr 1.2 m (range 1.0-2.0); p 0.45;
- vine Kcb scaled by multi-year July-August S-2 structural vigour, never by same-season stressed NDVI;
- cover-crop Kcb from S-2 in leafless windows (ingest 10 m stats for all months; SR is not needed);
- TAW prior from OpenLandMap or the French AWC map as a +-30% ensemble.
About 6 + 3 + 2 days.

4. Publish FTSW, Ks, deficit in mm, FTSW z, a stress index per phenological period, and days-to-stress. In the status, warning = soil, a new vine_stress field = modelled stress, and alert = vine_stress AND the existing S-2 canopy anomaly as the observed-impact confirmation. About 3 days.

5. Validate:
- at the 21 other SMOSMANIA stations (pre-registered metrics: dR with a paired bootstrap, onset MAE);
- retrospectively against cooperative harvest data and must delta13C;
- in a 2027 pilot (predawn potential plus ApeX on 3-5 parcels) that also calibrates per-parcel TTSW (Gaudin 2017 / Roux 2025).

Total is about 25 person-days. SR 2.5 m stays for maps only: it changes FTSW by at most 0.006.

evidence.finding:
Harmonic minus pooled ΔR:
- NDMI SR: +0.039 [+0.010,+0.065];
- CRSWIR SR: +0.047 [+0.019,+0.071];
- NDVI SR: +0.011 [-0.032,+0.048] in IV-X and +0.044 [-0.021,+0.094] in VI-IX.
Orbit-split minus pooled: -0.012 [-0.045,+0.015], so it removes the bias without hurting skill.

methods.validation_plan:
This is the validation layer itself. Write all thresholds down before computing:
- non-inferiority margin -0.03 for ΔR;
- 'added value' requires the lower CI bound of partial r given ERA5 to be > 0;
- parcel-truth criterion ρ(S-2 VII-VIII seasonal anomaly, δ13C) ≥ 0.52;
- repeatability across dry years (Kendall W of parcel ranks over 2019/2022/2026) reported alongside.

methods.description:
Extend validate_anomalies from Condom only to all SMOSMANIA stations already in data/7_isismn_data (21 stations, 5/10/20/30 cm, 2016-2024). Report an Atlantic/Gers stratum separately (CreondArmagnac, PeyrusseGrande, Lahas, Savenes, Urgons, Sabres, Montaut, SaintFelixdeLauragais) from the Mediterranean stations.

Per station, extract in GEE (reusing step_07 extract_satellite in a loop):
- ERA5-Land layers 1-3 (optionally layer 4);
- S1 GRD in a 50-100 m buffer, then change detection and SWI;
- SMAP L4 root zone.

Metrics, all pre-registered:
- anomaly Pearson R and Spearman rho, with each series' anomaly computed against its own day-of-year climatology over the common period;
- depth-matched pairs (ERA5 0-28 cm vs 20-30 cm) next to the operational 0-100 cm. This executes Z8 from F3;
- dekadal events POD/FAR/HSS with percentile-matched thresholds: the 16th percentile of each series over the common period, which removes the 1991-2020 vs 2016-2024 baseline mismatch in the current POD/FAR;
- extended triple collocation (McColl 2014; pytesmo.metrics.tcol_metrics) on (ERA5, S1-SWI, ISMN), giving each source's correlation with the unknown truth and the station's own representativeness error;
- pooled skill with a station-block bootstrap.

Add an in-situ quality flag for physically implausible low values. Condom 20-30 cm is below 0.10 m3/m3 on 27% of days, July-October, in a 41-46% clay soil; the likely cause is shrinkage cracks breaking sensor contact. Use rank (Spearman/percentile) metrics there.

methods.why_better_than_ndvi_only:
It is the only parcel-specific water signal that is not a vegetation-greenness proxy; ERA5 is one 9 km value for all 15 vineyards. It works through clouds every 1.5-6 days and responds to rain within hours, where NDVI reacts with a lag of weeks.

At Condom:
- the filter lifts Sentinel-1 from R=0.38 (raw) to R=0.555 vs 20-30 cm, close to ERA5 0-100 cm at 0.583;
- the partial correlation with the station given ERA5 is 0.21-0.30, so it carries information ERA5 lacks;
- its triple-collocation correlation with the truth is 0.71-0.77.

NDVI anomalies at the vineyard reach only R=0.43 vs the same reference.

issues:
Leftover limitations, all acknowledged by the author:
- one station under grass 136 m from the vineyard;
- probes at 20-30 cm while the vine root zone is about 1 m or deeper;
- Hargreaves with Tmax = 2*Tmean - Tmin (mean diurnal range 9.0 C, annual ET0 949 mm, P 733 mm);
- an absolute FTSW that is implausible (August RSW 0.09);
- only 9 year-blocks, so year-block CIs are rough;
- event CIs very wide, with 29 observed dry dekads in IV-X.
